  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the queen ant do?",
      "a": "The queen lays the eggs, sometimes thousands a day in big colonies, and her scent helps hold the colony together. She does not give orders; the workers organise themselves.",
      "asks": [
        "Is the queen ant the boss?",
        "How long does a queen ant live?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do ants decide which job to do?",
      "a": "Mostly by age, size and what they meet. Young workers nurse, older ones forage, and an ant that keeps meeting returning foragers with food, or finds a job undone, is more likely to switch to it.",
      "asks": [
        "How do ants know what to do?",
        "Do ants change jobs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do ants follow each other in a line?",
      "a": "A forager that finds food dabs a scent called a pheromone on the ground on her way home. Other ants follow the scent and add their own, so a good trail gets stronger, and an old one fades as the scent evaporates.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do ants walk in lines?",
        "What is a pheromone trail?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are soldier ants?",
      "a": "In some species, certain workers grow much bigger, with huge heads and jaws. They guard the nest, block entrances and, in some species, crack hard seeds. Leafcutter ants have several sizes of worker, each suited to different jobs.",
      "asks": [
        "Do ants have soldiers?",
        "Why are some ants bigger?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Are all worker ants female?",
      "a": "Yes. Workers and queens are female. Males are raised only at certain times; they have wings, fly off to mate with young queens and soon die.",
      "asks": [
        "Are there male ants?",
        "What do male ants do?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In many ant species, young workers tend the brood inside the nest and older workers forage outside.",
    "A black garden ant queen kept in a laboratory lived about 28 years."
  ],
